<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jáchymov</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Joachimsthal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jáchymov, in German Joachimsthal, is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Ore Mountains</a> of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czechia</a>, founded after rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> deposits were found there in 1516. The Counts Schlick minted large silver coins there from 1520, the Joachimsthaler, whose name, shortened to thaler, gave rise to the word dollar. Later its uranium ores yielded the radium isolated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-curie">Marie Curie</a>, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> prisoners were forced to mine uranium there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jachymov.jpg" alt="Jáchymov" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Devilsanddust · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J%C3%A1chymov_2016_a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jáchymov, in German Sankt Joachimsthal or Joachimsthal, lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Ore Mountains</a> in the Karlovy Vary District, about 15 kilometres north of Karlovy Vary on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The town stands in a valley at 733 metres, and its territory includes Klínovec, the highest mountain of the range at 1,244 metres. It was founded in a valley called in German simply Thal and was named after Saint Joachim, so that the name means Saint Joachim's Valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The village of Joachimsthal was founded by Steffan Schlick in 1516. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> caused the population to grow rapidly and made the Counts of Schlick one of the richest families in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. From 1520 they minted silver coins, the Joachimsthalers, called Thaler in German and tolar in Czech, from which, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> daalder, the word dollar derives. In 1528 Ferdinand I seized the right of mining. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgius-agricola">Georgius Agricola</a> based his metallurgical studies on observations made here in the late 1520s. In 1534, with about 20,000 people, Jáchymov was the second most populous town in the Kingdom of Bohemia, behind <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>.</p><p>The Church of Saint Joachim, built in 1534 to 1540, was the first Lutheran church in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> took effect in 1621 many Lutheran citizens migrated to nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> territory. The war and the lack of pumps to drain deep mines left the town unable to compete with cheap Spanish silver from the American colonies. Later Martin Heinrich Klaproth found that pitchblende could colour glass yellow, and discovered a new element in 1781, naming it uranium. The Austrian government sent 100 kilograms of waste from the uranium pigment factory to Marie Skłodowska-Curie and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie">Pierre Curie</a>, who found radium in it. Until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> this was the foremost source of radium in the world.</p><p>Jáchymov was annexed by Nazi Germany after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> in 1938, and most of its German population was expelled in 1945 and 1946. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> uranium mining began on a large scale, and almost all the uranium was exported to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Prison camps held prisoners of war, forcibly recruited citizens, political prisoners and opponents of the communist regime. At the peak in 1955 over 46,000 people worked there, 20 per cent of them involuntarily. Mining ceased in 1964.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jáchymov has about 2,200 inhabitants. The first radon spa in the world was founded there in 1906. The town is part of the Ore Mountain Mining Region, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and its historic centre is protected as an urban monument zone. The Royal Mint, built in 1533 to 1536, houses a museum of mining.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jáchymov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
